{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 560,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Boot.Dev for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some people worried that the large hadron \ncollider would smash particles together so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard it would make black holes that would swallow \nthe earth, open wormholes to other dimensions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. It didn’t and won’t. But the LHC \nmay be making a different kind of portal.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A portal to the dark sector. This isn’t \nanother name for the upside down. Rather,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s a hypothetical family of elementary \nparticles that exists in parallel to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "familiar particles of the standard model, \nbut are invisible to it. Invisible to us,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and therefore could be the answer to the dark \nmatter conundrum. And what is this portal?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42560,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it’s the particle that the LHC was built \nto find in the first place - the Higgs boson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've got a couple of quick announcements \nbefore we start. First, our eternal battle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the algorithm continues. The best way to \nencourage YouTube to share our videos is to like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and comment. Doing both really makes a difference. \nAnd if you're new here, subscribe, hit the bell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and introduce yourself in the comments. We're \nfriendly. Next up, we're excited to launch our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "composition of the universe nickel and enamel pin \nnow with a holofoil backing. If you want to flex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your knowledge of dark energy, dark matter, and \nthe terrifyingly small slice of ordinary matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you and our world is made of, check out our \nfirst pin of 2026. And if you prefer your mornings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fueled by dark energy, we've also got a brand new \ndark energy mug. Plus, our UV glow cur rotating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole hoodies and shirts are still available. \nLinks in the description. Now, onto the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs boson was discovered with the Large \nHadron Collider back in 2012. Physicists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "celebrated, a few collected their Nobel prize, and \nthey got back to work. They amped up the collider  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the expectation of discovering the particles \nthat would lead us to the next level of physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, supersymmetric particles that \nwould simultaneously explain the Higgs mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give us candidates for the dark matter particle, \nand even bolster string theory. No such particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appeared. OK, so maybe the undiscovered \nparticles are just so massive that there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143360,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn’t enough energy to grant them their mass. \nE=mc^2, so not enough “E” for the needed “m”.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we kept juicing up the collider, and \nkept finding nothing. At this point the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LHC is near its maximum planned energy—6.8 \nTeV in the most recent run and the design  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "goal is 7 TeV. So it’s unlikely that new \nparticles will appear before it maxes out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Disappointing? Maybe. But was it worth building \nthe LHC just to produce the Higgs? Absolutely.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Besides completing the standard model, it \ncould well be that the Higgs boson itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the key to the physics that lies beyond. And \nthat physics may finally explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now searches for dark matter have typically \nfocused on finding the dark matter particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as though a single add-on to the \nstandard model could solve the problem.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the best such candidates was the sterile \nneutrino, which Fermilab recently put to bed as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the simplest way out of the problem, as we \nrecently discussed. This might still work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218640,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but  … eh, it's getting dodgy. Or one of those \nundiscovered high-mass particles like predicted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by supersymmetry are dark matter—but again, \nthe LHC almost certainly won’t find those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, the parameter space for dark-matter \nas a single particle is getting worryingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrow. But just maybe the problem is that \ndark matter isn’t just a single particle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe there’s a whole family of particles \nthat exists in parallel to the standard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250160,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model that are all invisible to us—and \nby us I mean us standard model creatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How would that even work? In order to do the job \nof dark matter, the candidate particle needs to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be very difficult or impossible to detect \nvia interactions with the standard model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces—electromagnetism and the weak and strong \nnuclear forces. Their coupling to these forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to be weak or non-existent, leaving only \ngravity as the force linking those dark particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the standard model. In order to be invisible \nto the standard model, dark matter cannot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possess any of the standard model charges— so no \nelectric charge, no color charge, no weak charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can imagine single particles like this, but \nwe can also imagine an entire family of particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no coupling to the standard model. Such a \nfamily is called a dark sector, and its stable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles would be good dark matter candidates. \nNow just because such a dark sector doesn’t hold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard model charges, it may still have its \nown charges and its own internal interactions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It could even look a lot like the standard model. \nSo maybe we'd have dark quarks, dark leptons, dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325920,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bosons, that could interact to give dark protons, \neven dark atoms. Or its interactions might be very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333680,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different. Based on how dark matter appears to be \ndistributed in our universe, our hypothetical dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sector isn’t forming the same cool structures \nas the standard model matter. So there probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348160,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren’t any dark sector planets, or dark-sector \naliens in the room with you right now. Probably."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s the use of a dark sector? \nWell, its particles could be light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to already be within the energy \nrange of the LHC. Because they’re dark,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LHC detectors won’t spot them directly. But that \ndoesn’t mean we can’t notice their influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at what happens when two protons \ncollide in the LHC. You can think of protons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as tight bundles of several quantum fields. \nLike knots in which each thread has its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "internal weave. Collisions momentarily loosen \nthese knots, which can then retie themselves in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393040,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different forms. But not just any forms. Parts \nof the knot are undone or retied only in pairs;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weave of the threads—the symmetries \nof the fields—persist in the retying—in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the final particles. This means that \nconservation laws must be obeyed—energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and momentum and various charges—and also \nthe internal symmetries must be respected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A dark sector’s symmetries can look like \nthose of the standard model, but they come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from different gauge fields—different weaves \nto its threads—and that results in different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "types of charge that don’t “talk to” \nthe forces of the standard model. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obliterated protons don’t produce the \nfabric needed to make such particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not directly anyway. But there is \na way. If some of the debris of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the proton collision can settle down into \nthe simplest possible field configuration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the standard model—one lacking the \ncomplex symmetries—then that particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could go on to decay into dark sector \nparticles. Now the least “knotted” part  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463920,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the quantum field weave can be retied into \nanything respecting its simpler symmetries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More technically, only uncharged standard \nmodel field configurations—so-called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476640,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singlets—can couple to the dark sector quantum \nfields. We call them dark sector portals,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these field configurations are the only way \nto move energy between the standard model and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490000,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark sector. There are very few such portals. \nExamples include photon—dark photon mixing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498960,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hypothetical sterile neutrino and axion \ncouplings, and the Higgs field interactions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs may be the most promising of this \ngroup. Knowing that it exists and knowing how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512720,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make it is important. But the Higgs is \narguably the cleanest dark sector portal.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs field is a scalar field—the simplest \npossible quantum field—besides less knotted by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complicated symmetries, scalar fields are also \nuniquely good at coupling with other fields.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534240,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why the Higgs couples so widely to grant \nparticles mass. So, the expectation is that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs would also be great at coupling \nto other sectors, including dark ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great. Big takeaway: the Higgs \nboson can transform into dark sector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles. I’ll come back to how we actually \ndetect these, but this is already exciting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because although the LHC hasn’t produced lots of \nnew particle types, it can definitely make Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566800,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bosons. So then what’s it going to take to peer \nthrough the Higgs portal into the dark sector?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first is to help the LHC do \nwhat it does but even better:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579360,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce more Higgs bosons by increasing \nwhat we call the beam luminosity—that’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of collisions per second, which is very \ndifferent to the amount of energy per collision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The collider is currently switched off and in \nupgrade mode. When it turns on again in 2030,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the new “High Luminosity LHC” will produce \nfactor of 10 more collisions per second and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the following decade plus run is expected \nto produce around 380 million Higgs bosons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612880,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ten times more than all previous datasets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Producing a ridiculous number \nof Higgs bosons isn’t going to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be enough to find dark matter. We \nalso need to make another change:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to stop throwing away all of the \ninteresting data. In face we have probably already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632080,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trashed a ton of evidence for the dark sector \nif it exists. But don't judge we kind of had to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639280,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you collide protons at 99.99999905% the \nspeed of light, a lot of stuff comes out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the most part combinations of the well-known \nstandard model particles without even a Higgs in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mix. And with roughly 600 million collisions \nper second, the many-layered rings of detectors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662320,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the collision point are just strobing with \nactivity. And it's mostly boring activity. Finding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "signs of interesting physics—like the decay of \na Higgs boson—is like looking for a needle in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676320,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "haystack. And that's actually possible to do\ngiven enough time. But we don’t have time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming a modest 1 megabyte per collision, that \nmeans around a petabyte of data per second. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible to store it all for later inspection. \nWe need a way to very quickly sort the data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discard the noise, and save the interesting \nstuff. So, leaning into the needle-in-a-haystack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701280,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metaphor—it’s like trying to find \nthe single needle in every… I dunno,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704540,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1000 haystacks—when you have a haystack \nbeing thrown at your face every second.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can’t collect all haystacks—instead \nyou have a split second to decide whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to just duck each haystack or keep it for later \nanalysis. The ones you duck are gone forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719520,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So LHC experiments achieve this haystack sorting \nwith what's called a trigger. A given burst of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detector flashes is correlated with each other \nin ways that may match interesting physics, like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles you get from a Higgs boson decay. \nThose data are kept and everything else is thrown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. And this is all done in a split second. \nBut there’s an unavoidable issue—how do we you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know that you've encoded all possible interesting \nphysics into the way we set up those triggers?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if we set them to catch all the needles, \nbut the haystacks also contains diamonds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760640,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, enough with the metaphors. Let’s look at \nthe concrete example of detecting a Higgs boson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LHC detectors. These detectors are constructed in \nlayers. People like to say they're like an onion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but because they are cylindrical, \nso a leek? Details matter. Each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer is optimized for gathering different \nenergy from different particles. The inner-most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer detects how charged particles \nbend in a magnetic field. Then the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic calorimeter, which absorbs \nenergy from electromagnetic interactions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with particles like electrons and photons. \nThen the hadronic calorimeter which catches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything built out of quarks and gluons. And \nfinally the outermost layer is a muon detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now experimentally, muons are \nparticularly clean and easy to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identify at colliders. Their trajectories to \nthe point of detection can be reconstructed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as can the mass and momentum of \nthe particle that produced them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824480,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined with our triggers, this lets us really \nfine-tune our experiment to look for Higgs decays.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's because the Higgs boson is so  \nunstable that it doesn’t move far from its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creation point before falling apart. If we can \nreconstruct the trajectories of decay products,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843760,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we can with muons, we should find that they \ntrace back to the proton collision location.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851040,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this sort of trajectory origin for decay \nparticles is actually a standard cut that LHC  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments make in order to reduce the \namount of data that needs to be sorted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay now, so, let’s actually add the dark \nsector to this picture. There are lots of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ways to imagine a dark sector, but for the sake \nof this let’s just say that it arises from a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of parallel standard model with dark quarks, \ndark leptons, even dark photons etc. All of these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882800,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact via non-standard-model charges and so are \ninvisible except via a few portals like the Higgs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889680,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If this sector actually exists, then some Higgs \nbosons created in the LHC should decay into dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897280,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sector particles. For example, they could decay \ninto a dark quark—dark antiquark … or is it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark-quark—anti-dark-quark … anyway. Those quarks\nwill then do the same sort of things as regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarks—they'll become hadrons and mesons within \nthe dark sector—like, dark protons, dark pions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. Some of these will be stable and go off to \njoin the rest of the dark matter in the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But other dark sector particles will be \nunstable and they could decay back into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard model particles via another dark portal. \nFor example, they could decay into dark photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can become regular photons and then finally \nturn into something like a muon-antimuon pair."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that sounds like a complicated scenario, \nand it is. But the complication is the need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a portal both into and out \nof the dark sector. Besides that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is just the standard stuff that \nhappens after a particle collision,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to detectable particles that \nindicate the Higgs as an origin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our two examples of Higgs decay, we ended up \nwith muons. In the first case where the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decayed directly to muons, the trajectories of \nthe latter could be traced back to the proton  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision point. In the second case where an \nintermediate dark sector particle was produced,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s like the decay chain took a little \nsojourn through the upside-down before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "popping back into the world. And that means the \nfinal muons will have trajectories that trace  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to somewhere that’s displaced \nfrom the proton collision point.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe you can see the problem here. If \nwe’re throwing away detections based on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trajectory origin, then we’re throwing away all \nof these cases with a dark sector intermediary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That sounds like bad news. But the good news \nis that it’s conceptually straightforward to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fix this. When the high-luminosity LHC comes \nonline in 2030, it’ll do so with an update  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the trigger algorithm. To start with, this \nwould mean incorporating muons with displaced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "origins into the data scouting algorithm. \nNow data scouting means recording minimal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detail about events to build up a case \nfor storing more detailed data. In the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case of displaced muons—if there appear to be \na strong signal of these guys, we’ll go ahead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and record more detailed information for \nmuons originating off the collision point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Depending on the unknown properties \nof the hypothetical dark sector,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "significant evidence of displaced muons could \ncome within a year of the high luminosity LHC"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "switching back on. But it could also take \nseveral years if, say, the coupling is weak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the decay rates are inconvenient. Even \nthen, more work has to be done to build up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076800,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the argument that these displacements \nare due to dark sector intermediaries.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things like the mass distribution of the \nintermediary particles, their decay timescales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089520,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and various other things need to match plausible \nmodels. But if all of this comes together we'll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a frontrunner solution for problem of dark \nmatter--one of the most persistent conundrums in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of physics. And all we had to do was exactly \nwhat we were doing, just a bit smarter--watching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs bosons explode--but now echoed through \nthe dark sector and displaced though spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Boot.Dev for supporting PBS. Boot.dev \nknows that learning programming is challenging,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they believe you can learn to code by \ndoing what you’ll actually do at a job:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building real projects. Boot.dev uses \ntactics from modern game design to help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "students build real projects in Python, SQL and \nGo. On boot.dev you’ll be trained by a personal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI tutor named Boots (a wizard bear)** who will \nguide you as you earn XP, unlock Achievements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complete quests and fight bosses that help \nyou learn to code. You can use The Training  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1151120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Grounds - where you can grind infinite challenges \nbefore you forge ahead in your coursework. You’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158160,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also get access to the Boot.dev community which \nhas tons of real humans to help if you ever get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuck.. So if you’ve ever wanted to learn \nto code, Boot.dev is here. Go to Boot.dev  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171440,
    "dDurationMs": 21280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and use the code SPACETIME to get 25% off \nyour entire first year on the annual plan."
    } ]
  } ]
}
